    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most home treadmills run on a brushed DC motor, and brushes are the one part of that
          motor designed to be used up. They wear slowly over hundreds of hours, and when they get
          short enough the motor starts to hesitate, surge and struggle under load. On many motors
          they are reachable without taking the motor out. On some they are not, and either way
          the job comes with an electrical safety rule that is not optional.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Treadmill Motor Brushes: The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Motor brushes are spring-loaded carbon blocks that carry current to the
            spinning part of a brushed DC motor, and they wear down with use.</strong> The common
            signs of worn treadmill motor brushes are a belt that surges or hesitates under load,
            loss of power when you step on, the motor stopping mid-session while the console stays
            on, a change in motor sound, and in bad cases sparking or a sharp electrical smell.
          </p>
          <p>
            Replacement, on motors that allow it, is a short job: unplug and wait, remove the motor
            hood, unscrew the two brush caps on the motor body, slide the old brushes out, check
            them and the commutator they ride on, and fit an exact-match pair. Many treadmill motors
            are designed this way; others require the motor to come out, and some motors have no
            brushes at all.
          </p>
          <p>
            Brushes are not the first suspect for most power problems. Friction from a dry deck or
            an over-tight belt causes the same symptoms far more often, so check lubrication and
            tension before ordering parts. And if the commutator under the brushes is grooved,
            burnt or pitted, new brushes will not fix the motor; that is a motor-shop job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-motors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Which Treadmills Have Motor Brushes?</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Brushed DC motors do.</strong> They are the standard drive on most home
            treadmills because they are compact, inexpensive and easy to speed-control. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmill-motors-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long treadmill motors last</a>
            covers why brushes are one of the main wear items in that design.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>AC motors do not.</strong> The AC induction motors used in many commercial and
            high-end treadmills have no brushes to wear, which is part of why they tolerate heavy
            duty cycles. Our page on
            <a href="/commercial-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">commercial treadmills</a>
            covers where they appear.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Brushless DC motors do not either.</strong> Some newer machines use brushless
            motors, which switch current electronically rather than through brushes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>How to tell which you have:</strong> the manual or parts diagram is the reliable
            source. With the hood off, a brushed DC motor often has two round plastic or metal caps,
            usually slotted for a flat screwdriver, on opposite sides of the motor body near one end
            — those are the brush holders. The motor's own label may also describe it as a DC or
            permanent-magnet DC motor. If you cannot find caps and the manual does not mention
            brushes, do not go looking by dismantling the motor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-brushes-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What Motor Brushes Do and Why They Wear</h2>
<p>
            Inside a brushed DC motor the part that spins, the armature, needs current, and the
            current has to get onto something that is rotating. The commutator does that: a ring of
            copper segments at one end of the armature. Two carbon brushes press against it under
            spring pressure, one on each side, and current passes from the brushes into whichever
            segments they are touching as the commutator turns.
          </p>
          <p>
            Carbon is used because it conducts, it is slightly self-lubricating, and it is softer than
            copper, so the brush wears rather than the commutator. That is the design intent: the
            cheap part sacrifices itself to protect the expensive one. Each brush gets slowly shorter,
            and the spring keeps it in contact as it does.
          </p>
          <p>
            Wear speeds up with load and heat. Heavy users, long sessions at high speed and incline,
            and above all friction from a dry deck or over-tight belt make the motor draw more
            current, and more current means faster brush wear and more heat at the commutator. A
            well-maintained treadmill used moderately can go a very long time before its brushes
            matter. One that has run on a dry deck for years may need them much sooner.
          </p>
          <p>
            When a brush wears too short, its spring can no longer press it firmly enough, contact
            becomes intermittent, and the motor starts to misbehave — mildly at first, under load,
            and then more often.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="signs-of-wear" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Signs of Worn Motor Brushes</h2>
<p>
            Worn brushes cause intermittent loss of drive, and the pattern is gradual — usually
            developing over weeks rather than appearing overnight.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Surging or hesitation under load.</strong> The belt jerks or loses speed
            briefly as you land, then recovers, at a constant setting.</li>
            <li><strong>Loss of power.</strong> The machine holds speed empty but struggles with you
            on it, especially at higher speeds or incline.</li>
            <li><strong>Stopping under load.</strong> The belt stops mid-session while the console
            stays lit, sometimes with a motor-related error, and may restart after a pause.</li>
            <li><strong>Hard starting.</strong> The belt is slow to get going or needs a moment
            before it moves.</li>
            <li><strong>A change in motor sound</strong>, such as a new crackle, buzz or roughness
            that was not there before.</li>
            <li><strong>Sparking or a sharp electrical smell.</strong> Some small sparking at the
            commutator inside a brushed motor is normal and invisible from outside. Heavy arcing at
            worn brushes produces more, along with an ozone-like smell. If you can see sparks from
            outside the machine or smell burning, stop using it.</li>
            <li><strong>A lot of black carbon dust</strong> around the motor when the hood is off
            for the annual clean.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our guide to a
            <a href="/treadmill-shuts-off-by-itself/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill that shuts off by itself</a>
            covers the stopping symptom alongside its other causes, and the
            <a href="/treadmill-burning-smell/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">burning smell guide</a>
            covers what different smells usually mean.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rule-out-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Rule These Out Before Blaming the Brushes</h2>
<p>
            Every symptom above has more common causes, and brushes are rarely the first thing to
            check on a machine that is a few years old.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Friction.</strong> A dry or worn deck makes the motor work harder at every step,
            which produces exactly the same hesitation and loss of power. Lubricate first, following
            the manual and our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt lubrication guide</a>,
            typically every 40 to 50 hours of use.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Belt tension.</strong> A belt that is too loose slips; one that is too tight
            loads the motor. You should be able to lift it about two to three inches at the centre
            of the deck with the machine unplugged.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The drive belt.</strong> A slipping drive belt between motor and roller makes
            the running belt hesitate while the motor sounds normal. Our guide to the
            <a href="/treadmill-drive-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill drive belt</a>
            covers it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The controller board and speed sensor.</strong> Erratic speed with no relation to
            load, sudden total failure, or error codes point at the electronics rather than the
            brushes. Our page on a
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-slows-down-when-i-step-on-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt that slows down when you step on it</a>
            works through the load-related causes in order.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the machine is relatively new and lightly used, worn brushes are unlikely. If it has
            many years of heavy use, a brush check moves up the list.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="inspection" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. How to Inspect Treadmill Motor Brushes</h2>
<p>
            Only do this if your manual allows owner access to the motor compartment, you are
            comfortable working near mains electrics with the machine unplugged, and the motor has
            externally accessible brush caps. If any of those is not true, stop here and call a
            technician.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>1. Unplug and wait.</strong> Unplug at the wall and wait at least thirty minutes
            for stored charge in the controller to dissipate.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>2. Remove the motor hood</strong>, keeping the screws together, and take a photo
            of the compartment before touching anything. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-vacuum-treadmill-motor-compartment/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">vacuuming the motor compartment</a>
            covers hood removal and what to leave alone.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>3. Find the brush caps</strong> on the motor body. Clean dust away from around
            them so nothing falls into the holder when you open it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>4. Remove one cap at a time</strong> with a screwdriver that fits the slot
            properly — the caps are often plastic and easily damaged. The brush may push out under
            spring pressure, so keep a hand over it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>5. Ease the brush out</strong> by its spring or lead, noting which way round it
            sits. The worn face is curved to match the commutator, and if you refit the same brush it
            must go back in the same orientation.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>6. Check it.</strong> Compare its length against the minimum in the motor's
            documentation, a wear mark if it has one, or a new brush. Look for chips, cracks,
            uneven or burnt contact faces, a frayed or discoloured lead, and a spring that has lost
            its tension or looks heat-darkened. Any of those is a reason to replace both brushes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>7. Look into the holder</strong> with a torch at the commutator surface visible
            through it, as described under commutator condition below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="replacement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Treadmill Motor Brushes Replacement: The Steps</h2>
<p>
            Designs differ, so treat these as the general shape of the job and let your manual or
            motor documentation override any step.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Replace both brushes together.</strong> They wear at similar rates, and mixing a
            new brush with a worn one gives uneven contact.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Clean the holders.</strong> Vacuum carbon dust from around the caps and the
            motor exterior. Do not use compressed air, which drives carbon dust into the windings and
            bearings rather than removing it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Insert the new brush</strong> the correct way round, with the curved face matching
            the curve of the commutator, so it slides freely in its holder without forcing. If it
            binds, stop and check it is the right part and the right orientation.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Refit the caps</strong> by hand first, making sure the spring is seated and not
            twisted or trapped, then tighten snugly. Over-tightening cracks plastic caps.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Check nothing has moved</strong> against your photograph — wiring routing, the
            drive belt, the hood seating — then refit the hood with all its screws.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Bed the brushes in.</strong> New brushes have to wear to the commutator's curve
            before they make full contact. Follow the brush or motor maker's guidance; a common
            approach is to run the machine unloaded at a low speed for a while and keep the first
            few sessions gentle. Some extra noise or a faint smell at first can be normal while they
            seat; anything that persists or gets worse is not.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the symptoms are unchanged after new brushes have bedded in, the brushes were not the
            problem, and the next stop is a technician.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="matching-the-part" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Matching the Right Brush</h2>
<p>
            Motor brushes are not universal. They differ in width, depth and length, the shape of
            the contact face, the spring, the lead and terminal, and the carbon grade, and a brush
            that is nearly right can wear the commutator, bind in its holder or run hot.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Identify the motor, not just the treadmill.</strong> The motor has its own label
            with a manufacturer and part or model number, and that is what brushes are matched to.
            The same treadmill model may have shipped with different motors over its production run.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Order through the treadmill manufacturer first</strong>, quoting the treadmill's
            model and serial number and the motor label. Parts departments can confirm the right
            brushes, and some sell them as a pair with springs and caps. Specialist treadmill parts
            suppliers are the next option.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Be cautious with generic brushes.</strong> A brush with the same dimensions may
            still use a different carbon grade or spring. If you go generic, match every dimension
            and the lead arrangement exactly, and accept that it is a compromise.
          </p>
          <p>
            Brushes are usually an inexpensive part, often well below the cost of a service visit.
            If a parts supplier tells you brushes are not sold separately for your motor, the
            motor is designed to be replaced as a unit when they wear, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            is useful when comparing replacement motor ratings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="commutator" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Commutator Condition: When Brushes Are Not Enough</h2>
<p>
            The commutator is the copper ring the brushes ride on, and its condition decides
            whether new brushes are a fix or a waste of money.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Healthy:</strong> smooth, with an even brown or bronze film across the segments.
            That film is normal and helps the brushes work; it is not dirt to be scrubbed off.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Worrying:</strong> grooves or a ridge worn into the surface, burnt or blackened
            individual segments, pitting, a raised or loose segment, heavy black deposits, or
            visible damage between segments. Worn-out brushes that were left until their metal
            parts touched the commutator can score it badly.
          </p>
          <p>
            A damaged commutator needs a motor specialist, who can sometimes resurface it on a
            lathe, or a replacement motor. <strong>Do not sand it</strong> with sandpaper or emery
            cloth: abrasive grit is left in the gaps between segments, and emery is conductive.
            Do not spray cleaner into the motor either.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you can only see part of the commutator through the brush holder and what you see is
            bad, assume the rest is no better and get the motor assessed before buying brushes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-to-call" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. When to Call a Technician</h2>
<p>
            Call one, or the manufacturer, in any of these situations:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>The treadmill is under warranty and opening the hood could affect it.</li>
            <li>The brushes are not accessible from outside the motor.</li>
            <li>The commutator looks damaged.</li>
            <li>There is visible sparking from outside, a burning smell, smoke, or the breaker
            trips.</li>
            <li>New brushes do not fix the symptoms.</li>
            <li>You are not comfortable working near mains wiring and a controller board, even
            unplugged.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            A technician can check brushes quickly and test the motor and controller properly with
            instruments. That matters, because motor and controller faults overlap in symptoms and
            a motor that has struggled for months can damage the board. Our guide to a
            <a href="/treadmill-turns-on-but-belt-wont-move/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill that turns on but the belt will not move</a>
            covers how those faults differ.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="make-them-last" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. How to Make Brushes Last Longer</h2>
<p>
            Brush life is mostly a function of how hard the motor works, and most of that is under
            your control.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Keep friction low.</strong> Lubricate on schedule, clean under the belt
            periodically and keep tension correct. A motor fighting a dry deck draws more current on
            every step, and current is what wears brushes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Keep the motor cool.</strong> Vacuum the motor compartment annually, or every six
            months on carpet or with pets, and keep the floor under the front of the machine clean.
            Our
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance checklist</a>
            puts both on a schedule.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Respect the machine's rating.</strong> A motor that is undersized for the user's
            weight or training runs hot. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight capacity guide</a>
            covers the margins worth leaving.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use a proper outlet.</strong> A properly grounded wall outlet without an
            extension cord gives the motor the supply it was designed for.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the signs of worn treadmill motor brushes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Surging or hesitation under load, loss of power when you step on, the belt stopping mid-session while the console stays on, hard starting, a change in motor sound, and in bad cases sparking or an ozone-like smell. Symptoms usually build gradually over weeks. Friction from a dry deck causes the same signs more often, so lubricate first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I replace treadmill motor brushes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Unplug the treadmill, wait at least thirty minutes, and remove the motor hood. On motors with external brush caps, unscrew one cap at a time, ease the brush out noting its orientation, check the commutator, and fit an exact-match new brush the same way round. Replace both, refit the caps snugly, then bed them in gently. Follow your manual where it differs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do all treadmills have motor brushes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Brushed DC motors, which are common on home treadmills, have them. AC induction motors used in many commercial and high-end machines do not, and neither do brushless DC motors. The manual or parts diagram is the reliable way to check; on a brushed motor you can usually see two brush caps on the motor body.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do treadmill motor brushes last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on load, heat and maintenance rather than calendar time. Brushes wear slowly, and on a well-lubricated machine used moderately they can last a very long time. Heavy users, long high-speed sessions and running on a dry deck all increase current draw and wear them faster.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is sparking in a treadmill motor normal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A small amount of sparking at the commutator inside a brushed motor is normal and is not visible from outside the machine. If you can see sparks from outside, smell burning or see smoke, stop using the treadmill, unplug it and have it checked. Heavy sparking can mean worn brushes or a damaged commutator.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use generic motor brushes on my treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sometimes, but it is a compromise. Brushes differ in dimensions, face shape, spring, lead and carbon grade, and a near match can wear the commutator or bind. Identify the motor from its own label and order through the treadmill manufacturer or a specialist parts supplier first; if you go generic, match every dimension exactly.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With Treadmill Motor Brushes</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Brush jobs go wrong in predictable ways, and the first one costs more money than any of the others.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Replacing brushes to fix a friction problem</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Hesitation and loss of power under load are far more often a dry deck, an over-tight or slipping belt, or a slipping drive belt. Lubricate and check tension first; brushes are the suspect on high-mileage machines once those are ruled out.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Opening the hood too soon after unplugging</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The controller board can hold charge after the plug is pulled. Wait at least thirty minutes, never work with the machine plugged in, and never run it with the hood off.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Refitting a brush the wrong way round</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A used brush has a face worn to the curve of the commutator. Turned round, it makes poor contact and arcs. Note the orientation as it comes out, or replace both brushes with new ones.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sanding the commutator</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Sandpaper and emery cloth leave abrasive, and in the case of emery conductive, grit between the segments. A grooved or burnt commutator is a motor-shop job, and new brushes on it will not last.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

