        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Brushes wear by design</strong> on brushed DC motors, and worn
            ones cause surging, loss of power and stopping under load. Rule out friction and tension
            first; on a high-mileage machine with accessible brush caps, checking and replacing them
            is a short, inexpensive job.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Unplug and wait thirty minutes, match the part to the motor's own label, replace both
            brushes, and <strong class="text-white">call a technician</strong> if the commutator is
            damaged, you see sparks or smell burning, or the symptoms survive new brushes. Our
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmill-motors-last/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">motor lifespan guide</a>
